# Mount Command Automation Coverage

Status: in progress; baseline, shared conformance support, ordinary-widget
coverage, and native dynamic-input routing are complete.

## Purpose

Provide focused live DFHack evidence for every public DwarfSpec command whose
behavior depends on a mounted host, subject adapter, render owner, or input
target.

This checklist is intentionally narrower than a Cartesian product of every
command and every mount. A command is repeated only when the current
implementation crosses a distinct behavior boundary. Shared code paths receive
one representative live proof plus unit coverage for their adapters and
dispatch contracts.

## Current authoritative model

### Mount hosts

- `O`: ordinary `gui.View` mounted in DwarfSpec's owned screen.
- `W`: owned `overlay.OverlayWidget` mounted through its real overlay lifecycle.
- `S`: complete `gui.ZScreen`, supplied as either a class or an instance.
- `N`: non-owning native screen mounted with `ds.mountNativeScreen()`.

`ds.mount()` requires a component. A no-argument `ds.mount()` is invalid and is
not a native-screen shorthand.

### Subject sources

- The default source on `O`, `W`, and `S` is the mounted Lua component.
- The default source on `N` resolves native widgets from the borrowed base
  DF viewscreen and `df.global.game.main_interface`.
- `ds.ESubjectSource.NATIVE` explicitly selects that same native source.
- `ds.ESubjectSource.OVERLAY` selects a registered DFHack overlay by name.
- `native_root` is an advanced native-source option that bypasses ordinary
  root discovery. It needs direct live coverage, but it does not define another
  mount host.

Source selection changes subject lookup and inspection. It does not pin input
to the screen that supplied the subject.

### Native input and lifetime

- A native mount borrows the base DF viewscreen for lookup and observation.
- Every keyboard and mouse dispatch resolves the current top viewscreen at
  dispatch time.
- Showing, dismissing, and showing a fullscreen overlay therefore does not
  stale the mount or retained subjects.
- Consumed input stops at the current top screen. Unconsumed input falls
  through DFHack's normal viewscreen chain.
- `ds.unmount()` releases DwarfSpec's attachment and observers; it does not
  dismiss or otherwise own an existing overlay.

These contracts are already live-proven in
`tests/automation/native_input_routing_live_spec.lua`. They are not pending
recovery scenarios.

### Shared and mount-independent behavior

- `ds.capture_screen()` is run-level screen capture. It does not require a
  current mount and must not be repeated per host.
- `ds.await()`, `ds.wait_frames()`, `ds.getTick()`, `ds.getTime()`,
  `ds.hasFocus()`, `ds.getViewPos()`, `ds.setViewPos()`, and
  `ds.stage_overlay_registration()` are not mount-host matrix entries.
- Grid and pixel pointer spaces are interaction-layer behavior. Their shared
  live proof belongs in `interaction_live_spec.lua`; host specs only need to
  prove that a host supplies the correct target geometry.
- `O`, `W`, and `S` use the Lua view adapter. Root inspection and tree capture
  need not be repeated for all three unless host ownership changes the result.
- Native widgets and registered overlays use different subject adapters.
  Inspection and tree capture need representative live proof for both.
- `ds.current_run()` is runtime-only test instrumentation and is not declared
  in `src/ds.d.lua`. It is excluded from the public command inventory.

## Evidence rules

- Only focused live DFHack automation closes a host-, adapter-, render-, or
  input-dependent item.
- Unit, Lua syntax, package, process, cleanup, and live results are recorded
  separately.
- A passing command call is insufficient when the contract includes a visible
  state change, focus change, pointer move, render pass, handled/fallthrough
  result, or exact cleanup.
- Shared coverage is valid only when the implementation actually shares the
  adapter or dispatch boundary. Unit tests must protect that boundary.
- Tests must use DwarfSpec's public commands and real input lifecycle. They
  must not call production callbacks directly or leave global state, staged
  registrations, screens, overlays, observers, or pointer state behind.
- Add LuaDoc comments to every new or changed Lua function, method, class, and
  class-like test fixture.
- Use bounded waits with observable completion conditions and preserve
  unrelated worktree changes.

## Current command inventory

The declaration surface is 24 `ds` functions and 10 `Subject` methods.

Legend:

- `covered`: current live evidence exercises the relevant distinct boundary.
- `shared`: another listed live test covers the same current implementation
  boundary; no host-specific duplicate is required.
- `pending`: focused live evidence is still required.

### `ds` functions

| Surface | Current live evidence | Remaining distinct gap |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `ds.wait_frames()` | covered by shared scheduler/automation specs | none |
| `ds.await()` | covered by shared scheduler/automation specs | none |
| `ds.getTick()` | covered in `native_screen_mount_live_spec.lua` | none |
| `ds.getTime()` | covered in `native_screen_mount_live_spec.lua` | none |
| `ds.hasFocus()` | covered in `native_screen_mount_live_spec.lua` | none |
| `ds.getViewPos()` | covered in `view_pos_live_spec.lua` | none |
| `ds.setViewPos()` | covered in `view_pos_live_spec.lua` | none |
| `ds.mount()` | covered for `O`, `W`, and class/instance `S` | none |
| `ds.mountNativeScreen()` | covered in native mount and routing specs | none |
| `ds.root()` | covered for owned mounts, native widgets, and registered overlays | cover the `native_root` bypass |
| `ds.unmount()` | covered for owned and non-owning mounts | retain exact-cleanup assertions in new failure cases |
| `ds.get()` | covered for Lua, native-widget, and registered-overlay adapters | cover `native_root` lookup |
| `ds.inspect()` | covered for the Lua root and native subjects | directly inspect a registered-overlay subject |
| `ds.redraw()` | default redraw covered for `O`, `W`, and `N`; shared no-wait behavior covered for `O` and `N` | cover the distinct complete-screen render boundary |
| `ds.capture_view_tree()` | covered for `O`; shared by the owned Lua adapter | cover native-widget and registered-overlay trees |
| `ds.move_pointer()` | covered for the public top-level route, native-widget and registered-overlay bounds, and shared grid/pixel spaces | cover complete-screen geometry; no separate `W` case is required because its fluent hover already uses the same Lua bounds path |
| `ds.hover()` | covered for `O` and `W` | cover `S`, native-widget, and registered-overlay geometry |
| `ds.input()` | covered for `O`, `S`, and native dynamic routing | cover the distinct `W` input ingress; subject source does not create another input target |
| `ds.mouseInput()` | click/down/up/wheel covered for `O`; wheel covered for `W`; click/down/up/wheel and top-screen routing covered for `N` | cover the complete-screen physical mouse boundary |
| `ds.click()` | top-level route covered for `O`; Lua host behavior covered for `O`, `W`, and `S` | cover native-widget and registered-overlay bounds/dispatch |
| `ds.type()` | covered for `O`; complete-screen behavior covered through the fluent route | cover the distinct `W` and borrowed-native input targets, once each |
| `ds.viewport()` | covered for `O`, `W`, and `S` | prove native mounts reject owned-viewport mutation |
| `ds.capture_screen()` | covered as a mount-independent run command | none; do not repeat per host |
| `ds.stage_overlay_registration()` | covered by the registered-overlay native spec | retain exact registration cleanup in new registry cases |

### `Subject` methods

| Surface | Current live evidence | Remaining distinct gap |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `Subject:click()` | covered for `O`, `W`, and `S` | cover native-widget and registered-overlay targets |
| `Subject:hover()` | covered for `O` and `W` | cover `S`, native-widget, and registered-overlay targets |
| `Subject:move_pointer()` | covered for the Lua adapter, native widgets, and a registered overlay | cover the distinct complete-screen geometry |
| `Subject:input()` | covered for `O`, `S`, and native dynamic routing | cover the distinct `W` input ingress; no registered-overlay duplicate is required |
| `Subject:type()` | covered for `O` and `S` | cover `W` and the borrowed-native input target; native versus overlay source is irrelevant |
| `Subject:redraw()` | default redraw covered for `O`, `W`, and native widgets | cover the distinct complete-screen render boundary; source-specific and no-wait duplicates are shared |
| `Subject:inspect()` | covered for the Lua adapter and native widgets | directly inspect a registered overlay and verify native normalized scroll fields |
| `Subject:getFocusList()` | covered for the owned-screen and borrowed-native interaction models | cover the direct complete-screen focus boundary |
| `Subject:text()` | covered across the Lua adapter, a native row, and a registered overlay | none |
| `Subject:raw()` | exact identity covered for every current source adapter | cover the explicit `native_root` identity |

## Work removed by this revision

The following old tasks are no longer required:

- Treating fullscreen overlay show, dismiss, or reshow as native mount
  staleness. Current input routing deliberately follows the current top
  viewscreen, and live coverage already proves retained subjects remain usable.
- Remounting after ordinary fullscreen transitions. No recovery is required
  when the borrowed base screen and attachment remain valid.
- Repeating `ds.capture_screen()` for `O`, `W`, `S`, native widgets, and
  registered overlays. Capture is mount-independent.
- Repeating owned-root `ds.inspect()` and `ds.capture_view_tree()` solely
  because `O`, `W`, and `S` are different component types. They share the
  current Lua adapter.
- Repeating `Subject:getFocusList()` for both native widgets and registered
  overlays. Focus follows the borrowed native interaction model, not the
  subject lookup source.
- Treating every source as a separate keyboard/mouse dispatch target. Source
  options resolve subjects; dispatch still targets the current top screen.
- Testing component-source-option rejection independently for `O`, `W`, and
  `S`. One representative owned component is sufficient, backed by unit
  coverage of the shared rejection path.
- Duplicating every complete-screen case for both class and instance inputs.
  Both creation paths need lifecycle evidence, but command behavior can use one
  representative mounted screen.
- Repeating injected native observer install/restore failure combinations in
  live automation. Current unit coverage owns those synthetic failure paths;
  live coverage must instead verify the real observer leaves no residue.
- Counting undeclared `ds.current_run()` instrumentation as a public automation
  surface.

