- [x] `overlay_widget_mount_live_spec.lua` proves normalized Lua-list scroll
  inspection, overlay-first wheel handling, fallthrough, default fluent redraw,
  fullscreen preservation, and exact cleanup.

## Remaining implementation plan

### Phase 3: owned overlay widget

Extend `tests/automation/overlay_widget_mount_live_spec.lua` only at behavior
boundaries not already covered by the ordinary Lua adapter proof.

- [ ] Use a real editable/focusable overlay control to prove the overlay input
  ingress with one top-level keyboard route and one fluent text-entry route.
- [ ] Prove physical left click, down, and up through `ds.mouseInput()`; retain
  the existing wheel handled/fallthrough proof.
- [ ] Assert exact focus restoration, overlay cleanup, and unaffected backing
  input after the added cases.

### Phase 4: complete screen

Extend `tests/automation/complete_screen_mount_live_spec.lua` for behavior
unique to a directly mounted `gui.ZScreen`.

- [ ] Hover a rendered screen control and prove its complete-screen geometry
  through the public pointer lifecycle.
- [ ] Prove physical click/down/up/wheel handling and unconsumed fallthrough
  through the real screen chain.
- [ ] Prove the direct screen's exact focus list.
- [ ] Prove default fluent redraw while asserting the real complete-screen
  render counter/state. The shared no-wait branch is already covered.
- [ ] Retain one class case and one instance case for ownership and cleanup;
  run command behavior through one representative screen instead of
  duplicating the matrix.
- [ ] Assert exact pre-test screen restoration after success and failure.

### Phase 5: native widgets and registered overlays

Extend `tests/automation/native_screen_mount_live_spec.lua`. Do not re-test the
already complete fullscreen transition routing contract.

#### Native widget adapter

- [ ] Prove explicit `source=ds.ESubjectSource.NATIVE` root, lookup, and raw
  identity match the default native source.
- [ ] Prove `native_root` root identity, lookup, inspection, and cleanup without
  relying on ordinary root discovery.
- [ ] Capture a bounded native widget tree and assert stable semantic fields.
- [ ] Inspect a real native scroll control and assert the current normalized
  `scroll_position` and `visible_row_count` fields.
- [ ] Close the native bounds/dispatch routes with hover and logical click
  against a safely reversible target.
- [ ] Reject `ds.viewport()` without mutating native screen dimensions.

#### Registered overlay adapter

- [ ] Capture a bounded registered-overlay tree.
- [ ] Directly inspect a registered-overlay subject and prove hover and logical
  click. Pointer movement is already covered by the current staged overlay.
- [ ] Use a test-owned registered-overlay editable control to prove text entry
  through the borrowed-native input target. This single case covers the native
  mount's remaining type boundary; the source itself does not change dispatch.
- [ ] Assert the exact pre-test registry entry, visibility, focus, pointer, and
  screen state after cleanup.

### Phase 6: invalidation and failure cleanup

Cover invalidation that still exists in the current system, rather than
ordinary top-screen transitions.

- [ ] On one representative owned component, prove native/overlay source
  options fail deterministically and leave the component usable.
- [ ] Disable or remove a registered overlay after subject resolution; prove
  the retained subject fails with a stable diagnostic and a fresh `ds.get()`
  observes the current registry state.
- [ ] Replace a registered overlay entry; prove old and new identities are not
  silently conflated.
- [ ] Prove missing/disabled registry diagnostics do not mutate registration,
  visibility, focus, or the viewscreen chain.
- [ ] After representative registry and command failures, prove the real native
  observer, attachment, and cleanup registry have no residue.
- [ ] After each failure group, prove explicit unmount or a fresh mount still
  works and restores the exact pre-test state.

### Phase 7: closure

- [ ] Re-audit all 24 declared `ds` functions and 10 declared `Subject`
  methods against the current source before closure.
- [ ] Confirm each remaining matrix entry is either live-covered, explicitly
  shared across a verified implementation boundary, or documented as
  unsupported with a tested diagnostic.
- [ ] Run each focused host/source spec independently.
- [ ] Run the complete thin live wrapper once; do not use reload commands as
  test setup.
- [ ] Run the complete unit suite.
- [ ] Run the Lua syntax check.
- [ ] Report focused live, full live, unit, syntax, package/process, and cleanup
  evidence separately.
- [ ] Confirm no screens, overlays, registry entries, observers, render
  instrumentation, scheduled callbacks, focus changes, pointer changes, view
  position changes, or global mutations remain.

## Historical evidence ledger

These results record the completed groundwork. They are not a substitute for a
fresh closure run.

### Baseline

- Unit: 382 successes.
- Lua syntax: 224 files checked.
- Full live: 25 successes, zero failures/errors/pending.

### Shared conformance support

- Unit: 450 successes.
- Lua syntax: 234 files checked.
- Full live: 31 successes, zero failures/errors/pending.

### Ordinary-widget completion

- Focused ordinary live: 7 successes, zero failures/errors/pending.
- Full live: 34 successes, zero failures/errors/pending.
- Unit: 450 successes.
- Lua syntax: 234 files checked.
- Cleanup: confirmed by the focused and full live runs.

## Acceptance criteria

- Every current declared public surface appears in the inventory exactly once.
- Every remaining checkbox corresponds to a distinct current host, adapter,
  render, input, lifecycle, invalidation, or cleanup boundary.
- No task assumes native input remains pinned to the borrowed base screen.
- No task treats a normal fullscreen transition as mount staleness.
- No task duplicates mount-independent capture or shared Lua-adapter behavior
  without a documented distinct boundary.
- New live tests use real subjects and DwarfSpec's public input/pointer
  lifecycle, with exact restoration assertions.
- Focused live, full live, unit, syntax, package/process, and cleanup evidence
  remain separately reportable.
